    icon w5 size 44 set twemoji icon_look picture of globe in worlds hidden true
    icon w6 size 44 set twemoji icon_look picture of globe in worlds hidden true
    icon w7 size 44 set twemoji icon_look picture of globe in worlds hidden true
    icon w8 size 44 set twemoji icon_look picture of globe in worlds hidden true
    row worlds gap 22 fit.h 70 fit.w 640 fit.x 180 fit.y 175 hidden true
    scale lock cites ["watts2007"] y 330 font_size 30 label "Rank of Lockdown (1 = top)" min 1 max 48 value 40 ticks 5 markers [{"value":26,"label":"cold: 26th","color":"#8f887c"},{"value":1,"label":"world A: #1","color":"#2f6b8f"}] hidden true

    card title "Why do people follow the crowd?"

    hide lock_marker_1 lock_marker_2

Why do people follow the crowd? Start with a song almost nobody had heard: Lockdown, by a band called 52metro.
    draw note song

B: In 2006, Matthew Salganik, Peter Dodds and Duncan Watts put forty-eight songs by unkn (@draw lock@)own bands online, and more than fourteen thousand people came to listen and download.
    draw lab

Some heard the songs cold. The rest were split into eight separate worlds, each seeing how often others in their own world had downloaded every song.
    draw worlds

B: Heard cold, Lockdown was a middling song: twenty-sixth of forty-eight.
    draw lock_marker_1

    erase lab worlds

In one of the eight worlds it became the number one hit.
    draw lock_marker_2

    pause 0.3

    ask "In another of the eight worlds, where did Lockdown finish? Click the line." on lock store g default "12"
        right: {g.true}th: close to your {g}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it finished {g.true}th, near the bottom.

B: Same song, same kind of listeners. The only difference was  (@highlight lock@)who happened to download what, early on.
    highlight lock_marker_2

So one song could land almost anywhere, depending on the first few people.
    arrow span #b5482e from.x 100 from.y 268 to.x 756 to.y 268
    text same "same song" #b5482e x 440 y 232 font_size 34

    erase span same

The easy answer is that people are sheep: they copy, and copying turns luck into hits.
    text sheep "sheep?" #b5482e x 340 y 190 font_size 40

B: Maybe. But a download count is information. If you've never heard of any of these bands, isn't what others picked worth knowing?
    text info "or information?" #2f6b8f x 610 y 190 font_size 40

    pause 0.3

Then here's the puzzle: could every listener be perfectly sensible, and the crowd still end up almost anywhere?
    highlight sheep info effect box

    pause 0.5

## The urn game
sources:
    - id: andersonholt1997
      title: Information Cascades in the Laboratory
      authors: Anderson, L. R., Holt, C. A.
      year: 1997
      finding: 'American Economic Review 87(5): 847–862. Urn A held two light balls and one dark, urn B two dark and one light; six subjects per period each saw one private draw and announced a guess ($2 if right). Cascades formed in 41 of the 56 periods where an imbalance occurred; in one period the first two drew the misleading colour and all four later players followed despite their private draws.'
    - id: bhw1992
      title: A Theory of Fads, Fashion, Custom, and Cultural Change as Informational Cascades
      authors: Bikhchandani, S., Hirshleifer, D., Welch, I.
      year: 1992
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 100(5): 992–1026. Once the inferred evidence tips, it is optimal to ignore one''s own signal and follow; such cascades form quickly, can be wrong, and are fragile.'
      doi: 10.1086/261849
    - id: banerjee1992
      title: A Simple Model of Herd Behavior
      authors: Banerjee, A. V.
      year: 1992
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 107(3): 797–817. Sequential choices with private signals, told with two restaurants: people rationally follow those before them, and the herd can settle on the worse option.'
      doi: 10.2307/2118364

