    text fifty "50 : 50" #8f887c x 370 y 590 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "The urn game"

B: A download count mixes taste and information. To see the information alone, economists built a game: two urns and a die. The die secretly picks (@draw fifty@) one urn, fifty-fifty, and nobody is told which.
    draw uL uD die

Lisa Anderson and Charles Holt ran it in 1997. One urn holds two light balls  (@draw uDb1 uDb2 uDb3 uDt@)and a dark one; the other, two dark and a light one.
    draw uLb1 uLb2 uLb3 uLt

B: Six players, one at a time, draw a ball in private, put it back, and say out loud which urn they think it is. Tw (@draw pay@)o dollars for a right guess.
    draw p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 n3 q1 q2 q3 q4 q5 q6

Player one draws dark, and says dark urn.
    draw s1

    pause 0.3

B: Player two says dark too.
    draw s2

    erase pay

    hide q3

I'd trust my own ball. It's the only thing I actually saw.
    draw b3

    hide q1 q2 q4 q5 q6

    quiz "You're player three. Your ball is light; players one and two both said dark. Which urn do you name?" voice b
        right: The dark urn: their words reveal two dark balls, and yours is one light one.
        wrong: Each word before you reveals that player's ball. Count all three balls: here, the dark urn.
        * The light urn
        + The dark urn

    show q1 q2 q4 q5 q6

B: Count it. Their words tell you their  (@draw sc_line_2@)balls: dark and dark. Yours is light. (@draw sc_line_3@) Two against one: the dark urn is twice as likely.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

So I say dark, and my light ball goes unhea (@fade b3 to 0.35@) (@draw mute@)rd. Nobody behind me can tell what I drew.
    draw s3

    erase sc

B: Player four hears three darks, but knows yours tells nothing. Same spot as yo (@draw s5@)u: dark, whatever the ball. And s (@draw s6@)o on down the line.
    draw s4

That's an information cascade: once the evidence tips by two, everyone after rationally ignores their (@draw ban@) own. Sushi (@draw cred@)l Bikhchandani, David Hirshleifer and Ivo Welch named it in 1992; Abhijit Banerjee told it with two restaurants.
    draw casc

    erase ban mute

    fade b3 to 1

B: In one real round of their experiment, the first two drew the misleading  (@draw b1 b2 b4 b5 b6 parallel true@) (@hide q1 q2 q4 q5 q6@)colour, and all four after them followed, despite holding the right one.
    draw truth said

Four of six held the truth in their hands, and the line still said dark. Nobody was foolish. The information just never got out.
    highlight b3 b4 b5 b6 effect circle

    pause 0.5

## How often is the line wrong?
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: y = 100*(100-x)^2/(x^2+(100-x)^2)
    params:
      p: {value: 66.7, min: 50, max: 97, step: 0.5, label: ball quality p}
    x_range:
      - 50
      - 100
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 50
    x_label: p (%)
    y_label: Line locks on wrong urn (%)
    show_equation: false
    editable:
      - p
    drag: false
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: p}
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 150
      w: 720
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: andersonholt1997
      title: Information Cascades in the Laboratory
      authors: Anderson, L. R., Holt, C. A.
      year: 1997
      finding: 'American Economic Review 87(5): 847–862. Each private draw matches its urn with probability 2/3; it takes an imbalance of two inferred signals for a cascade to start (posterior 2/3 after two signals for and one against).'
    - id: bhw1992
      title: A Theory of Fads, Fashion, Custom, and Cultural Change as Informational Cascades
      authors: Bikhchandani, S., Hirshleifer, D., Welch, I.
      year: 1992
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 100(5): 992–1026. Cascades start early, can be wrong with substantial probability, and stop the aggregation of private information.'
      doi: 10.1086/261849

    text pcap "p = how often a ball matches its urn" #8f887c x 400 y 185 font_size 28 hidden true
    text ro "wrong: {eq.point_y:0}%" #b5482e cites ["andersonholt1997"] x 860 y 560 font_size 40 hidden true
    text oneinfive "1 in 5" #b5482e x 860 y 480 font_size 48 hidden true
    text stuck "100th player:\nstill stuck" x 860 y 370 font_size 32 hidden true
    text ext "informational\nexternality" #8a5fa8 cites ["bhw1992"] x 860 y 370 font_size 34 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mcard work [{"tex":"P(\\text{dark}) = 4/(4+2) = 2/3"},{"tex":"\\text{wrong} = (1-p)^2/(p^2+(1-p)^2)"},{"tex":"(1/9)/(4/9+1/9) = 1/5"}] x 420 y 430 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "How often is the line wrong?"

So a line of sensible people can lock onto the wrong urn. How often?
    draw axes

B: Here's the model's own arithmetic. Across the b (@highlight x_label pcap@)ottom, how good each ball is: one half is a coin toss, one is certain.
    draw pcap

I'd guess almost never. Two unlucky balls in a row can't be that common.
    highlight y_label

    quiz "With balls that match their urn two times in three, how often does a long line lock onto the wrong urn?" judge false voice b
        right: Watch the curve.
        * About 1 in 100
        * About 1 in 5
        * About 1 in 2

B: One line in fiv (@draw point ro oneinfive@)e. With balls this good, two early ones agree by bad luck more often than you'd think.
    draw curve_0

    pause 0.5

And a longer line doesn't help. The hundredth player is as stuck as the third: a hundred balls in a hundred hands, and the line only ever hears the first few.
    draw stuck

    erase stuck

B: Each player chooses well for themselves. What nobody counts is what their silence costs the people behind them. Economists call that an informational externality.
    draw ext

    erase ext oneinfive

Try it: drag how good the balls are, and find where wrong cascades become rare.
    explore

B: At nine in ten, about one line in a hundred goes wrong. Wea (@animate params.p 55 duration 2@)k signals, and the line is close to a coin toss.
    animate params.p 90 duration 3 ghost ["point"]

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

    fade curve_0 point axes ro point_ghost pcap to 0.15

For player three: two dark balls against one light one. That's four chances to two, or two thirds, for the dark urn.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

B: Balls come out in pairs until two in a row agree. A right pair has chance p squared; a wrong pair, one minus p, squared.
    draw mcard_line_2

At two thirds, that's one ninth against four ninths: one wrong line in five.
    draw mcard_line_3

    erase mcard

@test

    erase mathq

    fade curve_0 point axes ro point_ghost pcap to 1

So when is following the crowd rational? When others' choices carry evidence you don't have, and your own is weak. Every player in the line did the right thing.
    animate params.p 66.7 duration 2

    pause 0.5

## Smart sheep?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: No herd could lead you astray.
    good: You'd spot a cascade before it spots you.
    poor: The first two in line had you fooled.
    none: 'Fine: follow the crowd to the next lecture.'
sources:
    - id: bhw1992
      title: A Theory of Fads, Fashion, Custom, and Cultural Change as Informational Cascades
      authors: Bikhchandani, S., Hirshleifer, D., Welch, I.
      year: 1992
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 100(5): 992–1026. Cascades rest on little information and are fragile: a small public shock can overturn them, which helps explain fads.'
      doi: 10.1086/261849
    - id: andersonholt1997
      title: Information Cascades in the Laboratory
      authors: Anderson, L. R., Holt, C. A.
      year: 1997
      finding: 'American Economic Review 87(5): 847–862. Cascade behaviour in 41 of the 56 periods where an imbalance occurred; in a minority of cases subjects followed their own private signal against the Bayesian choice.'
    - id: weizsacker2010
      title: Do We Follow Others when We Should? A Simple Test of Rational Expectations
      authors: Weizsäcker, G.
      year: 2010
      finding: 'American Economic Review 100(5): 2340–2360. Across 13 social-learning experiments, players err in most cases where following others is optimal; on average they contradict their own signal only when the odds that it is wrong exceed about 2:1.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.100.5.2340
    - id: caichenfang2009
      title: 'Observational Learning: Evidence from a Randomized Natural Field Experiment'
      authors: Cai, H., Chen, Y., Fang, H.
      year: 2009
      finding: 'American Economic Review 99(3): 864–882. In a Beijing restaurant chain, showing diners the five most popular dishes raised demand for those dishes by 13 to 20 percent; naming sample dishes had no such effect.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.99.3.864

    text clean "following can be rational" #8f887c x 500 y 628 font_size 32 hidden true
    box c_frag "fragile:\none public fact tips it" #3d3833 cites ["bhw1992"] x 255 y 515 width 440 height 112 radius 10 font_size 34 hidden true
    box c_lab "lab: cascades in 41 of 56\nsome kept their own ball" #3d3833 cites ["andersonholt1997"] x 745 y 515 width 440 height 112 radius 10 font_size 34 hidden true
    box c_stub "too stubborn:\nswitch only at 2 : 1" #b5482e cites ["weizsacker2010"] x 255 y 380 width 440 height 112 radius 10 font_size 34 hidden true
    box c_bj "Beijing: top-5 dishes\n+13–20% orders" #2f6b8f cites ["caichenfang2009"] x 745 y 380 width 440 height 112 radius 10 font_size 34 hidden true
    text fit "or wanting to fit in?" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 285 font_size 38 hidden true
    text conf1 "cascades in the lab: mostly agreed" x 500 y 268 font_size 32 hidden true
    text conf2 "real herds, information or conformity: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 215 font_size 32 hidden true
    cards brk check each hidden true
        Breaks it: A trusted critic's public review
        Keeps it going: A hundred more join the queue
        Breaks it: Everyone's ball shown at once
        Keeps it going: A sign: most popular!
    icon shop size 90 set twemoji x 230 y 440 icon_look picture of "fork and knife with plate" hidden true
    icon qp0 size 80 set twemoji x 330 y 440 icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    icon qp1 size 80 set twemoji x 415 y 440 icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    icon qp2 size 80 set twemoji x 500 y 440 icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    icon qp3 size 80 set twemoji x 585 y 440 icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    icon qp4 size 80 set twemoji x 670 y 440 icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    icon qp5 size 80 set twemoji x 755 y 440 icon_look picture of "person standing" hidden true
    mark first2 around qp0 qp1 #b5482e kind box hidden true

    card title "Smart sheep?"

Rational, then. But does that make real crowds rational?
    draw clean

B: First, cascades are fragile. They rest on a couple of early choices, so one piece of public news can tip the whole line. Bikhchandani and coauthors saw fads that way.
    draw c_frag

In Anderson and Holt's lab, cascades formed in forty-one of the fifty-six rounds where the evidence tipped.
    draw c_lab

B: And in a fair share of (@highlight c_lab@) those moments, people kept their own ball anyway. Across thirteen such experiments, Georg Weizsäcker found people go against their own signal only when the odds against it reach about two to one.
    draw c_stub

So in the lab, people aren't too sheepish. If anything, they're too stubborn.
    highlight c_stub

B: Outside the lab, a Beijing restaurant chain showed diners its five most popular dishes. Those dishes sold thirteen to twenty percent more.
    draw c_bj

But a crowd can pull for other reasons too: wanting to fit in, or a bandwagon. The music worlds can't tell those apart from information.
    draw fit

    erase fit

How sure are we? That cascades happen in the lab: mostly agreed. How much of real herding, from hit song (@draw conf2@)s to market bubbles, is information rather than conformity: contested.
    draw conf1

    erase conf1 conf2

    fade c_frag c_lab c_stub c_bj to 0.3

So why do people follow the crowd? Often becau (@highlight clean@)se it's smart: other people's choices are evidence. The catch is that a crowd stops passing its evidence on.
    text final "others' choices = evidence" #2f6b8f x 500 y 290 font_size 46

    quiz "A new restaurant fills its tables with friends on opening night. What is it counting on?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Passers-by read a full room as evidence: to a stranger, a crowd is a signal.
        wrong: Ask what a stranger learns from a full room. Here, passers-by read it as evidence the food is good.
        + Passers-by read a full room as evidence the food is good
        * Crowded rooms let it charge more
        * Friends order more

    erase final

    hide c_frag c_lab c_stub c_bj clean

    draw brk

    ask "Which of these can break a cascade, and which keep it going? Sort each card." on brk store f stage own voice b
        right: {f} of {f.total} on the first try: only new public information, the critic's review or the balls shown, breaks a cascade.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try: only new public information, the critic's review or the balls shown, breaks a cascade.

    erase brk

    show clean final

    quiz "Five fund managers sell a stock, one after another. The sixth learns that managers two to five each saw the first one sell before deciding. How many independent signals does the line of sellers carry?"
        right: Perhaps just one: if the later four copied, only the first seller's own view is in the line.
        wrong: Ask whether sellers two to five used their own information or copied the first. Here, perhaps just one: the first seller's.
        + Perhaps just one: the first seller's
        * Five: one per seller
        * None at all

B: Next time a queue looks like proof, remember that it may rest on the  (@draw first2@)first two people in it.
    draw shop qp0 qp1 qp2 qp3 qp4 qp5 parallel true

    pause 0.5

